   "A": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to requiring state agencies to pay small businesses within fifteen days of receipt of an invoice",
    "basePrintNo": "S5283",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5283-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §179-f, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5283A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the state finance law, in relation to\nrequiring state agencies to pay small businesses within fifteen days\nof receipt of an invoice\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to ensure that small businesses are paid\nwithin fifteen days of receipt of an invoice for certain payments so\nthat they may continue to sustain themselves and conduct their\nbusiness activities.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends the prompt payment provisions of section 179-f of the\nstate finance law to ensure that small businesses are paid by state\nagencies within 15 days of receipt of an invoice for contracts other\nthan highway construction contracts, unless certain conditions exist\nto justify a delay in payment. Such conditions would be required to be\nrectified as soon as possible in order for small businesses to be paid\nin a timely manner.  Interest would start accruing on such payments\nwhen agencies do not provide payment to small businesses within 15\ndays.\n\nThis bill defines a small business as one whose primary place of\nbusiness is in New York state, which has a significant business\npresence in the state, that is independently owned and operated, not\ndominant in its field, and employs no more than three hundred\nemployees.\n\nThis bill would take effect on the 180th day after it shall have\nbecome a law, and would apply only to invoices received after such\ndate.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany small businesses have limited available funds to carry them from\neach respective pay period, let alone month to month.  Therefore, in\norder to ensure that small businesses and their employees are given\nthe best opportunity to succeed and sustain themselves, it is\nimperative to implement an accelerated prompt pay system to ensure the\nsustainability of these businesses. Shortening the prompt payment\nprovisions of the state finance law from 30 days to 15 days will help\nto facilitate meaningful and lasting participation of small businesses\non state contracts.\n\nState agencies would have six months to implement these changes to\ntheir prompt payment systems. Given the advances the state has made\nwith improving its business practices and processing of payments\nthrough the Statewide Financial System and other practices, this bill\nshould be able to be implemented smoothly in order to help small\nbusinesses succeed. Similar provisions have been successfully\nimplemented in other parts of the country.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect on the 180th day after it shall have become\na law and shall apply only to invoices received after such date.\n\n",
